<onlyinclude>'''Semiconductor packaging''' is the assembly and integration of one or more fabricated semiconductor dies into a mechanically supported, electrically connected and thermally managed component or system. Packaging connects die-level pads to external circuitry, protects interconnects and active material, and must preserve signal integrity and reliability under manufacturing and service conditions.</onlyinclude>

== Functions and boundaries ==
A package may contain a die attach, bond wires or flip-chip bumps, redistribution layers, a leadframe or laminate substrate, encapsulation, and a heat spreader. Its electrical path has resistance, inductance and capacitance; its thermal path governs junction temperature; mismatched thermal expansion can stress joints. Packaging follows wafer fabrication and die preparation but may involve wafer-level redistribution or bonding.

== Integration approaches ==
* '''Conventional packages''': wire-bonded or flip-chip die connected to external pins or solder balls.
* '''2.5D integration''': multiple dies placed side by side on an interposer or equivalent high-density routing structure.
* '''3D integration''': dies vertically stacked and interconnected through microbumps, hybrid bonds or through-silicon vias where applicable.
These are architectural descriptions, not universal manufacturing standards; individual implementations differ.

== Engineering and qualification ==
Design couples electrical parasitics, power delivery, thermal resistance, warpage, moisture exposure and interconnect fatigue. Qualification uses application-specific stress tests; IPC/JEDEC J-STD-020 classifies moisture/reflow sensitivity of nonhermetic surface-mount devices, but does not alone certify lifetime reliability.

